    {
      "name": "loss-average-meets-income-condition-first-limb",
      "note": "Art 5.5.1(b)'s FIRST limb read literally: the Average GloBE Income or Loss 'is a loss'. This is the vector that breaks if a loss year is coerced to zero.",
      "independent_oracle": {
        "source": "OECD GloBE Model Rules (Pillar Two), December 2021, Art 5.5.1(b) and 5.5.3(b); Commentary paras 84 and 91",
        "source_digest": "sha256:796d1a16fad360204a76450f5246e038263ef4bc652356f25d367d4b9389e306",
        "derivation": "Income years -3,000,000 / 500,000 / -800,000 sum to -3,300,000, average -1,100,000. That is a loss, so Art 5.5.1(b)'s first limb is met even though the magnitude exceeds EUR 1 million. Under a loss-to-zero coercion the average would instead be +166,666.67, so the reported average is the discriminating value. Revenue average = 12,000,000/3 = 4,000,000, less than 10,000,000, met.",
        "expect": {
          "average_globe_revenue_eur": 4000000,
          "average_globe_income_eur": -1100000,
          "average_globe_income_is_loss": true,
          "revenue_test_met": true,
          "income_test_met": true,
          "de_minimis_available": true,
          "deemed_zero_topup": true,
          "partial_window_used": false,
          "manual_review_required": false,
          "years_included": 3
        }
      },
